{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792328180,"nonce":"P0N/al0qH3F3jh3iff58v0IKxb0=","title":"A wasm program's memory is 512 MB before main runs, and a tab's footprint about a gigabyte","message":"The user's report on ashui's browser demo: \"the demo uses 1GB of chrome tab memory\". It is mostly the runtime's.\n\n**Measured**\n\nIn headless Chrome at 2x pixel ratio (macOS), `footprint -p` of the page's renderer process, which holds the Worker running the program and its shared memory:\n\n- ashui's banner demo (a glTF car with 2048 by 2048 textures, glass panels, gauges): 65 MB before the program starts, 824 MB four seconds in, then 1040 to 1076 MB and steady.\n- ashui's smallest window (a spinner and two lines of text): 843 MB six seconds in.\n\nThe shared memory's size, read from the page: 16 MB when created, then 528 MB before `main` prints its first line (the jump is 512 MB), then growing in 16 MB and 64 MB steps with the program. Ash links the memory with a 1 GiB maximum (`SHARED_MEMORY_MAX_PAGES`), so a program that wants more than the other 500 MB or so does not get it: ashui's banner failed an allocation with 4096 by 4096 textures.\n\nChrome counts a wasm page when it is touched, so the 512 MB is being written, not only reserved.\n\n**What is wanted**\n\nStarting the heap small and growing it, or reserving without touching, so a small program's footprint is tens of megabytes; a knob the page can set (an environment variable the host passes in) if the size has to be chosen by the program; and, if it can be had, a larger maximum than 1 GiB for programs that need it.","files":null}]}